    "name": "Cymatics",
    "description": "Sound builds the picture: a dark anodised square metal plate on a speaker driver, dusted with tens of thousands of bright warm-white sand grains, on a clean light lab bench under a strong soft key light. A tone plays and the sand dances (grains on antinodes hop, grains on nodal lines sit still) and gathers into the crisp geometric nodal-line figure of that frequency (Chladni figure, w = cos(n pi x)cos(m pi y) +/- cos(m pi x)cos(n pi y)); the pitch steps up and the figure shatters and re-forms into a more intricate one. Every grain's settled position per mode is precomputed (seeded Newton steps onto the nodal set + a little spread); motion is a closed-form blend with per-grain delay plus a hop proportional to the local plate amplitude. The plate flexes slightly with the mode shape. Sustained tones, a sand hiss and lock chimes come from the same mode list.",
